WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:10.550
MARKUS KLUTE: Hello and
welcome back to 8.701.

00:00:10.550 --> 00:00:14.610
So in this class we're going to
talk about the range of forces

00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:17.790
and specifically how
the range of forces

00:00:17.790 --> 00:00:20.250
depends on the mass of
the particle involved

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:23.140
in transmitting the force.

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:28.210
We have seen this table
before, the different forces,

00:00:28.210 --> 00:00:30.150
the strong force, the
electromagnetic force,

00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:33.340
and the weak force,
and the boson

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:39.570
which carries the force, gluons,
photons, and the W and the Z

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:41.610
bosons.

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:43.890
But now we want to
actually just look

00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:49.380
into the aspect of the range
and how masses interplay here.

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:52.170
So you all know the
electromagnetic potential

00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:54.730
due to a point charge given
by the Maxwell equation.

00:00:54.730 --> 00:00:59.040
We have seen this
equation before

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:02.370
and the time-independent
potential here.

00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:06.600
You also know that the
massless photon gives rise

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:10.745
to an infinite range of
electromagnetic [INAUDIBLE]..

00:01:10.745 --> 00:01:12.370
But the question now
is, how would this

00:01:12.370 --> 00:01:14.500
change, how would
this be modified

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:17.930
if the photon were massive?

00:01:17.930 --> 00:01:23.090
But to do this we have to
generalize just a little bit.

00:01:23.090 --> 00:01:26.690
First of all, we have to look
at the time-dependent equation

00:01:26.690 --> 00:01:28.280
or the wave equation.

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:31.480
And this is wave equation.

00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:35.230
You have also seen this before.

00:01:35.230 --> 00:01:40.660
Wave equation, and by
adding a mass term.

00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:44.500
So we are using here
an equation which

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:46.525
has to be fulfilled
by our particle

00:01:46.525 --> 00:01:49.540
simulation between energy,
momentum, and mass.

00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:53.470
We have discussed this in the
context of special relativity

00:01:53.470 --> 00:01:54.730
before.

00:01:54.730 --> 00:01:56.590
What we are trying
to do now is build

00:01:56.590 --> 00:02:00.190
a Schrodinger-like
equation by using

00:02:00.190 --> 00:02:03.810
the quantum mechanical operators
for energy and momentum.

00:02:03.810 --> 00:02:06.760
So we just add
this here and find

00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:09.924
a new equation, which is called
the Klein-Gordon equation.

00:02:18.350 --> 00:02:22.930
So this equation has to be in
all particle waves or particles

00:02:22.930 --> 00:02:26.390
have to fulfill the equation.

00:02:26.390 --> 00:02:28.930
So the question is,
what kind of solutions

00:02:28.930 --> 00:02:30.580
does this kind of equation have?

00:02:30.580 --> 00:02:32.240
How does this look like?

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:38.830
And if now start from, again,
a time-independent equation,

00:02:38.830 --> 00:02:42.500
you find solutions
which look like this.

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:44.420
And you see again
very similar, then,

00:02:44.420 --> 00:02:46.610
to before the charge
over some constant

00:02:46.610 --> 00:02:50.780
as a function of radius, but you
also see this exponential term.

00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:52.520
And what we see here
is this potential,

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:55.760
this form of potential is
called Yukawa potential.

00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:57.980
And what's nicely
shown in this plot here

00:02:57.980 --> 00:03:00.230
is, again, the potential
or the function

00:03:00.230 --> 00:03:03.410
of radius in units
of centimeters,

00:03:03.410 --> 00:03:06.170
the dependency of
the mass [INAUDIBLE]

00:03:06.170 --> 00:03:08.510
the range of the
force and the mass.

00:03:08.510 --> 00:03:11.960
So you see here as an example
for the mass equal to here.

00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:15.620
Mass is going to 1 GeV and
mass is going to 10 GeV.

00:03:15.620 --> 00:03:18.920
And you can easily see
the range of the force

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:23.270
is reduced by the fact that
the particle actually had mass.

00:03:23.270 --> 00:03:28.250
Now, the gauge boson, the boson
of the weak interaction, the W

00:03:28.250 --> 00:03:30.500
and the Z boson
are quite massive.

00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:33.140
They have masses in
the order of 80, 90,

00:03:33.140 --> 00:03:35.360
in the order of 100 GeV.

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:37.730
So you can see that
the masses actually

00:03:37.730 --> 00:03:41.810
leads to a reduction of
the range of this force.

00:03:41.810 --> 00:03:43.550
And yet, we find--

00:03:43.550 --> 00:03:45.230
here, just don't look.

00:03:45.230 --> 00:03:47.640
Don't look at the
gravitational part here.

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:49.750
It's not part of
the standard model.

00:03:49.750 --> 00:03:52.010
If you look at
electromagnetic interaction,

00:03:52.010 --> 00:03:54.500
where the range is
infinite, we find

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:56.190
that the range of
the weak interaction

00:03:56.190 --> 00:03:58.880
is 10 to the minus 18 meters.

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:01.430
So this is greatly
reduced, because

00:04:01.430 --> 00:04:03.200
of the masses of the particles.

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:06.710
If you just look at
the charge itself,

00:04:06.710 --> 00:04:09.710
we would find that
the weak interaction

00:04:09.710 --> 00:04:11.750
and the electromagnetic
interaction

00:04:11.750 --> 00:04:13.820
are actually quite comparable.

00:04:13.820 --> 00:04:15.920
We will also talk about
the strong interaction.

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:18.019
We see here there's
a strong interaction

00:04:18.019 --> 00:04:22.160
that the coupling, the
alpha, is in the order of 1.

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:24.080
And when we talked
about Feynman diagrams,

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:26.150
we talked about
perturbation theory.

00:04:26.150 --> 00:04:33.310
If you do a perturbation theory
for a interaction of order

00:04:33.310 --> 00:04:36.990
of 1, couplings of order 1,
your vertices on the order of 1,

00:04:36.990 --> 00:04:40.840
you will see that perturbation
theory might break down.

00:04:40.840 --> 00:04:41.550
So, OK.

00:04:41.550 --> 00:04:45.940
So what we have seen in this
lecture is how a mass, or how

00:04:45.940 --> 00:04:50.100
masses, reduce the
range of a force.

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:54.650
We have simply built
Klein-Gordon equation here,

00:04:54.650 --> 00:04:57.950
looked at a solution, and found
that there is this diminishing

00:04:57.950 --> 00:05:03.120
of the range of the force.

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:06.910
Later, we will look at one
additional complication,

00:05:06.910 --> 00:05:08.410
and another equation
which has to do

00:05:08.410 --> 00:05:09.785
with [INAUDIBLE]
particles, which

00:05:09.785 --> 00:05:11.940
is the so-called
Dirac equation, which

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:18.150
has to be fulfilled or
hold up by the fermions.

00:05:18.150 --> 00:05:20.610
But that's beyond the
scope of this class.

00:05:20.610 --> 00:05:23.520
We'll look at this
later in more detail.

